Title: THE LONG SHADOW
Credit: Written by
Author: TRUE STORY demo materials
Draft date: 2026
Notes: Original work created for demonstration. See demo/screenplay/README.md.

====

TITLE CARD: THIS IS A TRUE STORY.

FADE IN:

INT. AERODROME HANGAR - LINCOLNSHIRE - DAWN - 1936

Cold light through high windows. A single monoplane, half stripped.

MARGARET HOLLOWAY, thirty four, stands under the wing in a flying
suit two sizes too large. She does not look up when the door opens.

ARTHUR PENN enters. Fifties, overcoat, a folded newspaper under one
arm. He watches her work for a moment before he speaks.

ARTHUR
They're saying you'll not make Karachi.

MARGARET
They said that about Cairo.

ARTHUR
You didn't make Cairo.

MARGARET
I made Cairo twice.

She wipes her hands. On the hangar wall behind her, a large poster
for the 1935 Schneider Trophy, the lettering scorched at one corner.

ARTHUR
Margaret. You've never flown a night leg alone.

MARGARET
I flew Croydon to Le Bourget in November. In the dark. Alone.

INT. AERODROME OFFICE - CONTINUOUS

A cramped room. A wireless set. A wall of charts.

ARTHUR
The Air Ministry refused her a licence in 1931. Did you know that?

He is speaking to CHIEF ENGINEER DAVIES, sixty, who does not
look up from a ledger.

DAVIES
I knew it.

ARTHUR
Twice. They refused her twice.

DAVIES
She was never refused. She withdrew.

ARTHUR
That is not what the record says.

DAVIES
Then the record is wrong, and you are repeating it.

Davies closes the ledger.

DAVIES (CONT'D)
She was the first woman to hold a first class navigator's
certificate in this country. In 1933. Look that up before you
print anything.

EXT. AERODROME - DAY

The monoplane is pushed into the light. A small crowd. A boy
holds a camera he does not know how to use.

Margaret climbs the wing. Arthur calls up to her.

ARTHUR
One question. For the paper.

MARGARET
One.

ARTHUR
Why alone?

MARGARET
Because the second seat is weight.

She drops into the cockpit. The engine catches.

INT. NEWSPAPER OFFICE - NIGHT

Arthur types. Behind him, a wireless plays "The Way You Look
Tonight," Fred Astaire. Someone turns it down without being asked.

EDITOR (O.S.)
Have you got her?

ARTHUR
I have got half of her.

EDITOR (O.S.)
Which half?

ARTHUR
The half that is documented.

He looks at his own last line and strikes it out.

INT. HOLLOWAY COTTAGE - NIGHT

Small, warm, cluttered with charts. Margaret's sister CATHERINE
sets down two cups. A framed photograph on the mantel: three
children on a beach, badly faded.

CATHERINE
Mother told everyone you were in Cairo.

MARGARET
I was in Lincolnshire.

CATHERINE
I know where you were.

A pause. The wireless is on low in the next room.

CATHERINE (CONT'D)
She was an impossible woman and you have her temper exactly.

MARGARET
That is not a fact, Catherine, that is an opinion, and you have
been repeating it for twenty years.

CATHERINE
It is both.

EXT. THE COAST - DAWN

The monoplane, small against a great deal of sky.

INT. AERODROME OFFICE - DAY - LATER

Arthur, older by a decade in the way he holds himself. Davies
is gone. A younger clerk, MISS ELLIS, files.

ARTHUR
The Karachi record still stands in her name?

MISS ELLIS
It stands. Nobody has taken it.

ARTHUR
And the 1931 business?

MISS ELLIS
There is no 1931 business. There never was. You wrote it and
then everybody had it.

Arthur says nothing for a long moment.

ARTHUR
Then I should like to correct it.

MISS ELLIS
You are about forty years late.

ARTHUR
I am aware.

He picks up a pen.

FADE OUT.

TITLE CARD: Margaret Holloway's Karachi record stood for
thirty one years.

TITLE CARD: The correction ran on page nineteen.

THE END
